<div class="space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight lg:text-5xl mb-4">
            <span class="text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-amber-400 animate-gradient-x">
                Authentication
            </span>
            <span class="text-white">with JWT and Passkeys</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            Implement secure, stateless authentication for your APIs and web applications using JSON Web Tokens, and phishing-resistant sign-in with <a href="#passkeys" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">passkeys</a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Overview Section -->
    <section>
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-900/20 to-orange-900/20 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-white/10 shadow-xl backdrop-blur-sm">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <div class="p-3 bg-amber-500/20 rounded-lg text-amber-400">
                    <svg class="w-8 h-8" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3 mt-0 border-none">Secure by Default</h2>
                    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-0">
                        SoliLang includes first-class support for JWT (JSON Web Token). Unlike session-based auth, JWTs are stateless, making them perfect for microservices, mobile apps, and modern Single Page Applications (SPAs).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Creating Tokens -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">1</span>
            Creating Tokens
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Use the <code class="text-amber-300">jwt_sign</code> function to create a signed token. You can include any custom claims in the payload.
        </p>
        
        <!-- Terminal Window -->
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-green-500/80"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">controllers/auth_controller.sl</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># 1. Define your payload
payload = {
  "sub": "user_12345",
  "name": "Alice Wonderland",
  "role": "admin",
  "iat": now
}

# 2. Get your secret key securely
secret = getenv("JWT_SECRET")
# 3. Sign the token (default algorithm: HS256)
token = jwt_sign(payload, secret)
# Option: Set expiration (e.g., 1 hour from now)
token_with_exp = jwt_sign(
  payload, 
  secret, 
  { "expires_in": 3600 }
);</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Verifying Tokens -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-orange-500/20 text-orange-400 text-sm border border-orange-500/30">2</span>
            Verifying Tokens
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Verify incoming tokens using <code class="text-orange-300">jwt_verify</code>. This function checks the signature and expiration automatically.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">middleware/auth.sl</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">result = jwt_verify(token, secret)
if result["error"] == true
  # Handle invalid token
  print("Auth Error:", result["message"])
else
  # Token is valid, access claims
  user_id = result["sub"]
  print("Authenticated User:", user_id)
end</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Security Warning -->
    <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-yellow-400 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z" />
            </svg>
            Security Warning
        </h4>
        <p class="text-yellow-200/80 text-sm">
            Never hardcode your <code>JWT_SECRET</code> in your source code. Always use environment variables (<code>.env</code>)
or a secure secrets manager. If your secret is compromised, all issued tokens become insecure.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Passkeys -->
    <section id="passkeys" class="scroll-mt-20">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">3</span>
            Passkeys
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Passkeys (WebAuthn) replace the password with a key pair the user's device keeps: the server stores only a
            public key, and a signature over a fresh challenge proves possession. They cannot be phished &mdash; the
            browser binds every response to the site's origin &mdash; and there is nothing to leak or reuse.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-generate" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Generate it</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth --passkeys</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">
            On a new app this writes session auth and passkeys together; on an app that already ran
            <code class="text-amber-300">soli generate auth</code> it adds only the passkey files. The flag goes before or
            after the folder, and no existing file is overwritten:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">File</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it does</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/models/passkey_credential.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>PasskeyCredential</code> (one row per passkey) and the challenge helpers</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/controllers/passkeys_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>GET /passkeys</code>, <code>POST /passkeys/options</code>, <code>POST /passkeys</code>, <code>DELETE /passkeys/:id</code> &mdash; the signed-in user's passkeys</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/controllers/passkey_sessions_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>POST /passkeys/login/options</code>, <code>POST /passkeys/login</code> &mdash; sign-in</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/views/passkeys/index.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">List, add, remove</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/views/passkeys/_sign_in.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The sign-in button, for <code>partial("passkeys/sign_in")</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>public/js/passkeys.js</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The browser side, no dependency</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>db/migrations/…_create_passkey_credentials.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Unique index on <code>credential_id</code>, index on <code>user_id</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            On a fresh app the sign-in view already renders the partial, and its email field has
            <code class="text-amber-300">autocomplete="username webauthn"</code> so the browser also offers passkeys in its
            autofill (conditional UI). An existing sign-in view is left alone: add
            <code class="text-amber-300">&lt;%- partial("passkeys/sign_in") %&gt;</code> and the <code>autocomplete</code>
            value yourself. Run <code>soli db:migrate up</code>, sign in, and add a passkey at <code>/passkeys</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-config" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Relying party and origins</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Variable</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Default</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold"></th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">WEBAUTHN_RP_ID</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the host of <code>APP_BASE_URL</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The domain passkeys belong to; set the parent domain (<code>example.com</code>) to share them with <code>app.example.com</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">WEBAUTHN_ORIGINS</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>APP_BASE_URL</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Comma-separated, exact origins (scheme, host, port) a ceremony may come from</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the RP ID</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Shown by the browser</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Browsers allow passkeys on <code>https://</code> and on <code>http://localhost</code> only. The default
            <code>APP_BASE_URL</code> (<code>http://localhost:5011</code>) works in development as long as you browse that
            exact origin; behind a proxy, set <code>APP_BASE_URL</code> to the public <code>https://</code> URL.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-sign-in" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">How sign-in works</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><code>POST /passkeys/login/options</code> returns request options with an empty <code>allowCredentials</code> (discoverable credentials: no username needed) and keeps the challenge in the session under <code>webauthn_challenge</code>, tagged with its purpose and an expiry (<code>WEBAUTHN_CHALLENGE_TTL</code>, 300&nbsp;s).</li>
            <li>The browser signs it; <code>passkeys.js</code> posts the credential's <code>toJSON()</code>.</li>
            <li><code>POST /passkeys/login</code> takes the challenge out of the session <em>before</em> verifying (a replay or a second tab fails closed), finds the passkey by <code>credential_id</code> with <code>find_by</code>, verifies it with <code>WebAuthn.verify_authentication</code>, and checks that the user handle is present and names the same account.</li>
            <li>It then opens the session exactly as the password login does: the per-IP throttle (<code>AUTH_ATTEMPTS_PER_IP</code>), the locked and confirmed gates, <code>session_regenerate()</code>, <code>user_id</code> and <code>session_version</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Every failure &mdash; unknown credential, bad signature, expired challenge, locked account, a sign counter that
            went backwards &mdash; gets the same <code>401</code>, so the endpoint reveals nothing about which passkeys exist.
            The JSON requests carry the page's CSRF token in <code>X-CSRF-Token</code> (the views emit it in
            <code>data-csrf-token</code>), so they also pass with <code>SOLI_CSRF_TOKENS=require</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-native" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Hand-rolled ceremonies</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The generated code is a thin layer over the native <code class="text-amber-300">WebAuthn</code> class &mdash;
            <code>registration_options</code>, <code>verify_registration</code>, <code>authentication_options</code>,
            <code>verify_authentication</code> &mdash; documented in
            <a href="/docs/builtins/webauthn" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">Built-ins &rarr; WebAuthn</a>.
            Use it directly for an API or a different storage model.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout-warning">
            <p class="text-yellow-200/80 text-sm mb-0">
                <strong>Limits.</strong> Attestation is never verified (the options ask for <code>"none"</code>), so passkeys
                cannot be restricted to certain authenticator models. ES256 and RS256 are supported; EdDSA is not yet.
                Passkeys are an additional credential: an account still has a password.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>